-- 1898_md_member_page_no_instrument_and_composed_citations.sql
-- The Maryland member-page cohort -- the largest single source class left in the national
-- stance-sourcing audit. 82 keys blanked, 20 blocked, and migration 1897's Steve Johnson
-- evidence CORRECTED in place.
--
-- 🔴 WHY A FORWARD WRITE. Season 1 is CLOSED and IMMUTABLE. A blank is `value 0` INSERTED into
-- the OPEN season, never a DELETE -- a deleted Season 2 row falls back to Season 1 and the bad
-- chair stays visible (CC_0057). Every blank CARRIES THE SOURCE IT EXAMINED (migration 1887).
--
-- ══ THE HEADLINE: 70% OF THIS COHORT IS SOUND, AND THAT IS THE FINDING ═══════════════════════
-- The recorded backlog called this "932 MD member-page keys". Measured fresh against production
-- the voter-visible, still-seated, member-page-ONLY set is 521 keys across 88 legislators. Every
-- one was tested against that member's OWN sponsorship record, read per session from mgaleg
-- (Members/Details/<slug>?ys=<session>, which is mgaleg's own attribution, so the surname twin of
-- md_sponsor_identity_traps §2 cannot bite). 448 sponsorships for Steve Johnson alone; 2023RS,
-- 2024RS, 2025RS and 2026RS for all 88.
--
--   MATCHED           254   the row quotes a bill title the member really sponsored
--   PARAPHRASE_MATCH  113   the row paraphrases one -- verified by contiguous token run
--   NO_INSTRUMENT      89   the row names no bill, no vote, nothing
--   VOTE_CLAIM         35   the row claims a VOTE; a sponsorship index cannot test it
--   PARTIAL/other      30   weak overlap, or outside the sessions read -- owed a human
--
-- 🔑🔑 367 OF 521 ROWS (70%) CARRY A VERIFIED SPONSORSHIP. The generic-source detector flagged
-- this whole class because the URL is a roster page -- but for most of these rows the roster page
-- IS the evidence, because it lists the member's bills and mgaleg attributes each one. So the
-- right disposition for the bulk of the MD backlog is a CITATION REPAIR (cite the bill page),
-- NOT retirement. A "932-key backlog" is mostly a citation-format problem, not an evidence
-- problem, and treating it as a retirement queue would have destroyed hundreds of true rows.
--
-- ⚠⚠ THAT CONCLUSION SURVIVED FOUR PASSES, EACH OF WHICH RESCUED ROWS THE PREVIOUS ONE WOULD
-- HAVE BLANKED. Pass 1 matched Title-Case spans only: 162 NO_INSTRUMENT. Pass 2 added contiguous
-- token runs for paraphrases and moved 113 out, 64 of them straight out of the blank list --
-- April Miller's and April Rose's "solar energy construction restrictions in priority
-- preservation areas" IS HB0460, and blanking them would have destroyed a true claim. Pass 3
-- added reordered paraphrases and bill numbers. Pass 4 was hand-reading all 72 survivors. The
-- first cut over-fires; it always does.
--
-- ══ WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG, CLASS 1: NO INSTRUMENT (69 written, 19 blocked) ══════════════════
-- A roster page states who someone IS -- name, party, district, committee -- and lists the bills
-- they put their name to. IT CARRIES NO POSITION. These rows name no bill, no vote and no
-- statement, and reach a chair from party, district or role alone: "Backed progressive revenue
-- measures and tax equity legislation", "As a Baltimore City Democrat ... supports comprehensive
-- clean energy legislation", "Supported civil rights and anti-discrimination legislation across
-- multiple sessions". 🔑 A PARTY IS NOT AN INSTRUMENT -- the rule that blanked the Morales and
-- Campos redistricting rows in 1897. Concentrated in taxes (25), public-safety-approach (24) and
-- civil-rights (11): the topics where a party label feels most like an answer.
--
-- ══ CLASS 2: COMPOSED BILL CITATIONS (13 written, 1 blocked) ═════════════════════════════════
-- 🔴🔴 A REAL BILL NUMBER WELDED TO AN ACT NAME THAT DOES NOT EXIST. 29 rows in the cohort cite a
-- bill number -- the most checkable thing a stance row can contain. 14 check out. 15 do not, and
-- they fail in the worst possible way: the number resolves to a real Maryland bill about an
-- entirely different subject.
--   * "Maryland Voting Rights Act (2023) HB 1261" is cited by FOUR legislators -- Ben Barnes,
--     Joseline Peña-Melnyk, Mary A. Lehman and Nicole A. Williams -- all seating voting-rights at
--     CHAIR 1 on it. HB1261 (2023RS) is "Workplace Fraud and Prevailing Wage - Violations -
--     Penalties and Referrals", sponsored by Delegate Valderrama. None of the four is on it.
--   * "HB 996 (2023) Abortion Care Access Act" is "Artificial Intelligence - Implementation
--     Causing Injury or Death - Civil and Criminal Liability".
--   * "HB 1171 (2023) Abortion Care Access Act" is "Baltimore County - Speed Monitoring Systems".
--   * "HB 1221 (2024) child care subsidy eligibility" is "Battery Storage and Solar Arrays Safety
--     Training Grant Program and Fund".
--   * "HB 1121 (2024) clean energy workforce development" is "Public Health - Opioids and Opioid
--     Overdose Reversal Drugs - Information".
-- 🔑 AND THE SUBTLER HALF: three of them cite a bill the member REALLY DID SPONSOR and invent its
-- subject. Veronica Turner's HB0478 (2026) is hers -- it is "Income Tax - Subtraction
-- Modification for Classroom Supplies Purchased by Teachers", not "Immigration Enforcement
-- Agreements - Prohibition" (that is HB0444). Her HB0347 is hers -- it is "Workers' Compensation
-- - Occupational Disease Presumptions - Hypertension", not a "Voting Rights Act of 2026".
-- Valderrama's HB0997 is hers -- "State Finance and Procurement - Prevailing Wage Rate", not
-- "Real Property - Short-Term Rentals". THE NUMBER CHECKS OUT AND ONLY THE SUBJECT IS INVENTED,
-- so the citation looks maximally verifiable and is false on inspection.
--
-- ⚠ ONE ROW IN THAT SET IS **NOT** A DEFECT AND IS LEFT ALONE: Mary A. Lehman / housing cites
-- HB 693 (2024), which is the Renters' Rights and Stabilization Act of 2024 -- real housing
-- legislation -- and SHE IS AMONG ITS SPONSORS. Ben Barnes's row cites the same bill but he is
-- not a sponsor and his row claims only that he "supported" it, so his is blanked and hers is
-- kept. The same citation, two different verdicts, decided by reading the sponsor list.
--
-- ══ 🔴🔴 THE BUG THAT NEARLY PRODUCED A MASS FALSE FINDING ═══════════════════════════════════
-- The first run of the bill-number check took the session only from a parenthesised year like
-- "(2023)". Claims are written "(SB0977 2025)" just as often, so the year was missed and the
-- fallback tried 2023RS FIRST. MARYLAND RENUMBERS EVERY SESSION, so that compared a 2025 bill
-- against the unrelated 2023 bill sharing its number -- and reported a mismatch every single
-- time. It briefly looked as though almost every cited bill number in Maryland was fabricated.
-- The fix takes the year NEAREST the number, then any year in the text, and records which session
-- was actually used in every verdict. 🔑 WHEN A CHECK FAILS FOR EVERYTHING, SUSPECT THE CHECK.
--
-- ══ CORRECTING MIGRATION 1897 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 1897 blanked Steve Johnson's eight chairs correctly -- he is a Democrat whose rows called him
-- an "Eastern Shore Republican" -- but its stated evidence OVERCLAIMED THE WINDOW. It said his
-- page "lists 150 bills for the 2025 and 2026 sessions"; a member page shows the CURRENT session
-- only, so that reading covered 2026RS alone. All four sessions of his term have now been read
-- (117, 118, 123 and 90 sponsorships), and 1897's own control count "Solar 0" turns out to be an
-- artefact of the one-session window: he co-sponsored HB0300 (2023RS) "Solar Panels on Schools"
-- and HB1233 (2025RS) "Community Solar Energy Generating Systems", both EXPANDING solar -- the
-- opposite of the restrictive siting bill his row attributed to him. The claimed bill is still
-- not his: HB0460 (2026RS) "Solar Energy - Construction of Generating Stations in Priority
-- Preservation Areas and Study" is co-sponsored by Delegates April Miller and April Rose, both
-- REPUBLICANS, and Johnson is not among them -- which independently confirms 1897's finding that
-- his rows carried a Republican's record. The eight blanks stand; their reasoning is rewritten
-- here because it is shown to voters.
--
-- ══ BLOCKED: THE SAME ORPHAN TOPIC, FOR THE THIRD MIGRATION RUNNING ═════════════════════════
-- 20 keys cannot be written because `immigration` has NO Season 2 pin. Added to the three left by
-- 1896 and the three by 1897, **26 defective chairs now stand unfixable on that one topic.** A
-- pre-guard below fails loudly if immigration ever gains a Season 2 pin.
--
-- ⚠ NOT FIXED HERE, DELIBERATELY: the 35 VOTE_CLAIM rows need MD roll calls, which a sponsorship
-- index cannot stand in for, and the 30 PARTIAL/out-of-window rows need a human and, for members
-- seated before 2023, sessions earlier than the four read here. Both are recorded in the
-- dispositions file beside this migration.

